WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.590
One of the occupational hazards of doing a
show like this is that my friends, my family,

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:15.290
people who come up to me at the grocery store
or at IKEA, and pretty much everyone I encounter

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.490
wants to talk politics with me, in one fashion
or another.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:21.940
Fortunately, I love talking politics and talking
to people about politics, so I'm usually happy

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:26.460
to engage with people even when I'm picking
out an ice cream or or testing out the Kivik

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.789
couch, which is surprisingly comfortable and
easy to put together.

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:33.750
One thing that I've really noticed in our
current political climate is people are having

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:39.159
a harder time tuning politics, even briefly,
to talk about other things.

00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:44.950
This is somewhat anecdotal in my case particularly,
but I'd bet it's happening in your life, too.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.770
Suddenly, it seems like every conversation
is political, every discussion somehow gets

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.380
back to Trump, and yes, your aunt knows exactly
how to solve America's health care issue,

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.500
even though she can't figure out how to sync
her iPad.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.420
On face value, none of this is a problem.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.190
I love people who were largely apolitical
are becoming more engaged.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.940
People are not only talking talking about
politics, but in many cases reevaluating what

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.320
their beliefs are because of the upside down
world that we will in right now.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.470
While everything may seem crazy right now,
I really do believe this time we're in right

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:24.430
now is a rare, special moment when so many
people are all doing the same introspection

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.460
about their beliefs all at the same time.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:33.460
Whether this will lead to more or less freedom,
tolerance, or peace all remains to be seen.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.470
But without question we're in some pretty
fertile ground for change right now.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:42.770
And by change I mean real societal change,
not just hope and change.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.410
One problem I do see attached to all this
opportunity for change and reevaluation is

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:52.280
that people are becoming consumed by politics
in a way which doesn't strike me as particularly

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.780
healthy for a human's mental health, or for
our society's at large.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.620
It's one of the reasons I'm a small government
guy.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.741
I want a government small enough that we don't
have to talk about it that much because it

00:02:02.741 --> 00:02:06.630
doesn't have the power to do too much, good
or bad, to our lives.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:11.260
There's a built in trade off there...small
government can only do so much, the rest is

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.620
up to the people.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.950
So if you want government to do everything,
you really can't complain when it does all

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.810
sorts of things you don't like.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:23.810
Well, actually of course you can complain
about it, at least until that big government

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.200
takes your right away to do just that.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.340
So my message to you this week is: let's keep
talking about all of these issues, let's keep

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:34.860
staying engaging and trying to change things
for the better, but at the same time let's

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.500
not let it consume us to the point where it
changes who we are.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.901
Social media can be an awful catalyst to the
endless focus on politics, which is why I've

00:02:43.901 --> 00:02:48.170
been trying to take the weekends off and focus
on the world that exists in front of my face

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:51.360
instead of the one that exists in 140 characters.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.850
When you are online though, try to find some
nuance between Trump Derangement Syndrome

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.260
and Nazi Frog obsession.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.819
Most people are good and want pretty much
the same things that you do, so let's keep

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:05.810
growing that new center.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.910
And as long as your here right now, we're
once again partnering up with Learn Liberty

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:13.709
this week and we've got author and director
of the philosophy, politics and economics

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:16.569
department at Duke University, Dr. Mike Munger.

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:17.910
We're gonna talk politics, philosophy, economics
and more.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.519
Watch and learn, unless it's the weekend,
in which case I'll give you the day off.

